      id: "case-01",
      title: "Kaveri Polymers: build or buy capacity",
      text: "Kaveri Polymers Ltd is a listed manufacturer of engineering plastics based in Gujarat, with FY 2025-26 revenue of ₹1,200 crore and EBITDA of ₹168 crore. Its two plants ran at 92% capacity utilisation, and the extrusion line at the Vapi plant is the recognised bottleneck. Kaveri's shares trade at an enterprise value of about 10 times EBITDA.\n\nThe board is considering two routes to grow. Option 1 is a greenfield plant costing ₹400 crore that would take three years to commission. Option 2 is to buy 100% of the equity of Sundar Plastics Pvt Ltd, an unlisted competitor selling similar products to the same customers. Sundar's EBITDA is ₹65 crore and the sellers want an enterprise value of ₹520 crore. Kaveri would pay ₹380 crore in cash for the equity on completion, plus a further amount after two years if Sundar's EBITDA exceeds ₹80 crore; the fair value of this earn-out at the acquisition date is ₹20 crore. Kaveri's valuers estimate the fair value of Sundar's identifiable net assets at ₹310 crore. Sundar's workforce, processes and customer contracts would all transfer.\n\nThe combined market share would be significant in two product categories, and the parties expect the deal to cross the thresholds for notifying the Competition Commission of India. During preliminary due diligence, the team learnt that the state pollution control board has issued a show-cause notice to Sundar about effluent discharge, which Sundar's management describes as 'routine'.\n\nAt the Vapi bottleneck, Kaveri makes two grades. Grade X sells at ₹200 per kg with material cost of ₹120 per kg, and the line processes 4 kg of it per minute. Grade Y sells at ₹300 per kg with material cost of ₹150 per kg, and the line processes 2 kg of it per minute. Other factory costs are largely fixed in the short term. The CFO wants to know which grade to prioritise until new capacity arrives."
