      id: "case-04",
      title: "Narmada Agro Exports: currency and concentration",
      text: "Narmada Agro Exports Ltd, a listed company in Madhya Pradesh, processes and exports rice. Exports form 85% of its revenue, invoiced in US dollars, and about 70% of export sales go to buyers in a single Gulf country. A wholly owned subsidiary in Dubai, Narmada Trading FZE, buys rice from the Indian company and resells it to retail chains in the region.\n\nOn 1 February 2026 Narmada shipped rice worth USD 2 million to an unrelated buyer on 90-day credit, when the spot rate was ₹83.00/USD. The closing rate on 31 March 2026 was ₹83.60/USD.\n\nFor a new USD 5 million receivable due in three months, the treasurer has these quotes: spot ₹83.20/USD; three-month forward ₹83.90/USD; and a three-month USD put option with a strike of ₹83.50/USD at a premium of ₹0.40 per dollar (ignore the time value of the premium).\n\nThe company exports under a Letter of Undertaking and accumulates input tax credit on packaging, transport and milling services. Its rice attracts no export duty. Its tax team is also reviewing the price at which rice is sold to the Dubai subsidiary, which earns a markedly higher margin than unrelated distributors in the region. The statutory auditor has flagged the same sales for attention in the standalone audit.\n\nEarlier this year the government briefly imposed a minimum export price on certain rice varieties, which hurt volumes. The board wants to reduce dependence on one region and is evaluating entry into West African and European markets with its existing rice range.\n\nNarmada's working capital is funded by packing credit in foreign currency, and its board has a policy of hedging at least half of confirmed export receivables. Competitors from other Asian exporting countries have been cutting prices in the Gulf market."
